[Music]  mit has this  insane abundance of talented musicians  about half the mit undergraduate  population is taking music courses every  year  and they're these crazily impressive  people  when the shutdown started looming in  that kind of final week  in march we really started to realize  just  how important the immediacy of being  together was  and what it meant to us to be making  music in the same place  at the same time  so we got them all microphones and we  shipped them off all over to everybody  in the orchestra  and we had actually performed a piece of  brian eno's music  called discreet music in the fall which  is basically an  improvisation where everybody chooses  their own tempo in their own patterns  and supposedly was just to teach them  how to get the right levels  and how to like learn how to position  their mic and stuff like that but really  was to kind of make a piece out of this  like a collage  so i got these 45 tracks and i put them  all together  and suddenly we had made an orchestra  piece together  and i let them know that we're going to  have the first zoom meeting  exactly when the first rehearsal would  have been and like i just opened up the  program  and all these faces started popping up  you know all these kids that i've been  working with all year and i just got so  happy  in a way that i hadn't been really since  the whole thing began you know and they  seemed really happy to see each other  they seemed really happy  just to connect and i just realized this  is  kind of about education and it's kind of  about music but it's also just about  [Music]  connecting  then it was just like well what should  we do next and so  uh we decided that we would uh take on  beethoven that way  so we all find it hilarious that i  walked into this like  trying to do everything but play  beethoven with this orchestra  you all hear that but then it turned out  that the second movement of the seventh  symphony was the perfect project in this  situation  it had just the right sort of feeling  for the moment because it's  sort of funerial in a weird way but it  has these kind of moments of hope in it  it goes up a couple steps and then it  stays there so  we'll finish it on the last day of term  but there ain't nothing like the real  thing right so  it's not gonna sound like the vienna  philharmonic you know  but the other ensembles are doing  similar things the jazz band is working  that way the wind ensembles working that  way  it's been great and what's exciting to  me about coming back together is that  we'll have what we've learned from this  experience  which is actually quite a lot of  information about who we all are as  musicians and what our skills are i mean  i talked to my colleagues about  how we kind of know more about our  students  individual abilities than we did going  in because i'm  getting 40 recordings from them a week  i felt a little bit guilty about the  fact that it had to by necessity  turn into a recording techniques class  so that's not what they signed on for  they just wanted to come to an orchestra  and playing an orchestra a couple times  a week  but it was empowering things for them to  know  and i realized it helps them in exactly  the ways that  they're supposed to be helped as  musicians if you use this in the right  way then you can play more in tune and  you can play more in time and you can  actually hear  why it sounds different so what we've  gained from that technology  we can use it and put it back where it  belongs which is  as this tool for togetherness and  connection and empathy  and to be able to do that in a space  that's actually designed  to make music it's kind of like when  biologists  are doing the best they can to do their  work at home but ultimately when they  get back in the lab  they have all their equipment and they  have everything designed in the right  way they have the right temperature  control the right clean rooms  then they can really do their work and  so for us  we're in this holding pattern and we're  making the best of it and we're learning  things but  what we're really gonna do with that is  what happens when we actually get back  together  in a real music space  you

